          %h3#CwR Categories with Representable Maps
          %p
            Taichi Uemura introduced
            %span.newWikiWord
              categories with representable maps
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/categories+with+representable+maps"} ?
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+semantics"} functorial semantics
            approach to type theory generalizing the notion of a Category with Families/Natural Model. A category with representable maps (CwR) is a category with finite limits with a specified class of “representable” arrows that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback-stable"} pullback-stable
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentiable+morphism"} exponentiable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+morphisms"} representable morphisms
            in the usual sense. Then any CwR can be considered to be a type theory: for instance, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free"} free
            CwR with a representable map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tm
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ty
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm \to Ty
            has as models precisely the natural models, and so can be thought of as the embodiment of the judgments of Martin-Lof Type Theory (with no connectives).
