          %h2#examples Examples
          %p Comprehension categories and display map categories are easy to come by “in nature”, but it is more difficult to find examples of the “split” versions of the above structure (which are what is needed for modeling type theory). Here we summarize some basic known constructions.
          %p However, first we should mention the examples that come from type theory itself.
          %h3#syntactic_categories Syntactic categories
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
              of any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
              has all of the above structures. Its objects are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
              in the theory, and the types in context
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
