                    is a terminal object.
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Substitution
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    Pullback along
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    Application of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi f
                            %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    Application of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %p
              The next column describes the same CwF in the terminology of slice categories: Every object of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              corresponds to a slice category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{/ \Gamma}
              over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{/ \Gamma}
              is also lcc. Every morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \Gamma \rightarrow \Delta
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              induces a pullback functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi Δ
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^* : \mathcal{C}_{/ \Delta} \rightarrow \mathcal{C}_{/ \Gamma}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
              preserves the finite limits and dependent products of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{/ \Delta}
              (i.e. it is an lcc functor), and the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi 𝒞
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi Δ
                                  %mo *
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↘
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi Γ
                                  %mo *
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi 𝒞
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                                %mi Δ
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo *
                              %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi 𝒞
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                                %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; \mathcal{C}
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}_{\Delta^*} \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow_{\Gamma^*}
                    \\\
                    \\mathcal{C}_{/ \Delta} &amp;&amp; \stackrel{\to}{f^*} &amp;&amp; \mathcal{C}_{/ \Gamma}
                    }
            %p
              commutes (up to unique isomorphism). Conversely, every lcc functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Δ
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{/ \Delta} \rightarrow \mathcal{C}_{/ \Gamma}
              under
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is uniquely isomorphic to the pullback functor along some morphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo →
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \rightarrow \Delta
            %p
              The last column describes the
              %strong big model
              of type theory in the opposite category of lcc categories and lcc functors. Since the contexts of this model are itself models of type theory, it can be understood as a “model of models”. The definition of the big model can be arrived at by removing all reference to the fixed lcc category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              in the previous column. Instead of just the slice categories of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a terminal object in the slice category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{/ \Gamma}
              and is thus generalized to a terminal object in any lcc category.
            %p
              The usual model in a fixed lcc category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              can be recovered from the big model by slicing: The contextual core (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ContextualFromAttributes"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is equivalent to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %p
              However, the naive definition of the big model above suffers from the same coherence issues as the standard models in individual lcc categories: Lcc functors preserve lcc structure (i.e. type formers) up to isomorphism, but not necessarily up to equality. These coherence problems can be resolved by working with a suitable model categorical presentation of the
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2, 1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              presenting a higher category is unique up to zig-zag of Quillen equivalences, but that the underlying 1-categories of these model categories can vary non-trivially. Because coherence issues are ultimately about equations in the underlying 1-category, we can thus hope that some model categories presenting the category of lcc categories will be better behaved than others for our purpose.
            %p
              One possible way to construct such a well-behaved model category is as follows (see (
              %a{:href => "#Bidlingmaier20"}> Bidlingmaier 2020
              ) for details):
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  First one defines a model category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lcc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Lcc}
                  of lcc sketches. An
                  %strong lcc sketch
                  is a category with some diagrams
                  %a{:href => "#Isaev2016"} marked
                  as finite limits cones and evalution maps of dependent products. These marked diagrams do not need to satisfy the corresponding universal property, however. The model category structure is set up such that every object is cofibrant, and the fibrant objects are precisely the lcc categories with diagrams marked if and only if they satisfy the corresponding universal property. Thus the subcategory of fibrant objects of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lcc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Lcc}
                  corresponds to the usual category of lcc categories. Note however, that lcc categories in the sense of fibrant lcc sketches “have” finite limits and dependent products in the sense that these universal objects
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %em merely exist
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lcc
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Lcc}
              %li
                %p
                  The model category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{sLcc}
                  of
                  %strong strict lcc categories
                  is defined as the category of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+fibrant+objects"} algebraically fibrant objects
                  of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lcc
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Lcc}
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lcc
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Lcc}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lcc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Lcc}
                  correspond to distinguished choices of universal objects, and these choices are preserved by the morphisms of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{sLcc}
                  = succeed "." do
                    %strong strict lcc functors
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{sLcc}
                  supports substitutions that preserve type formers up to equality.
                %p
                  However, it does not support some dependent type formers in any obvious way, notably not
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
                  types. The problem is that the context extension
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo .
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma.\sigma
                  of some
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ∈
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Ob
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \in \operatorname{Ob} \mathrm{sLcc}
                  by a type (i.e. object)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                  is given by freely (in the 1-categorical sense) adjoining a morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \rightarrow \sigma
                  to
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
                  types, one has to relate
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo .
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma.\sigma
                  with the slice category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_{/ \sigma}
                  in some way, and it appears that this is not generally possible. Note that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_{/ \sigma}
                  has the universal property of a context extension in the higher/bicategorical sense, but that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo .
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma.\sigma
                  is defined purely 1-categorically.
              %li
                %p
                  To reconcile context extensions
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo .
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma.\sigma
                  with slice categories
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Γ
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %mi σ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_{/ \sigma}
                  %a{:href => "#ChingRiehl2014"} algebraically cofibrant objects
                  of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{sLcc}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{sLcc}
                  is a coalgebra for a fixed cofibrant replacement comonad. The category of such coalgebras has model category structure, and this model category is again Quillen equivalent to
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{sLcc}
                %p
                  A cofibrant object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{sLcc}
                  has the property that every non-strict lcc functor (i.e. morphism of underlying lcc sketches) out of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                  is isomorphic to a strict lcc functor. This property turns out to be sufficient to construct a weak equivalence
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo .
                        %mi σ
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi Γ
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma.\sigma \rightarrow \Gamma_{/ \sigma}
                  in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{sLcc}
                  %em algebraically
                  cofibrant
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} sLcc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{sLcc}
                  with CwF structure that supports finite limit,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
                  type constructors.
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+category"} fibration category
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clan"}> clan
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tribe"} tribe
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type-theoretic+model+category"} type-theoretic model category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initiality+conjecture"} initiality conjecture
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Awodey%27s+conjecture"} Awodey's conjecture
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+of+type+theory+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} model of type theory in an (infinity,1)-topos
          %h2#References References
          %p
            The general idea of categorical semantics of dependent types goes back at least to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Seely84"} Seely 1984
            %em
              categories with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/display+maps"} display maps
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clans"}> clans
            ”) is due to:
          %ul
            %li#Taylor87
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"}> Paul Taylor
                , §4.3.2 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Recursive Domains, Indexed Category Theory and Polymorphism
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Taylor-IndexedCategoryTheory.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Taylor99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"}> Paul Taylor
                , Section 8.3 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Practical+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Practical Foundations of Mathematics
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.paultaylor.eu/~pt/prafm/index.html"} webpage
          %p General overview:
          %ul
            %li#Hofmann97
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hofmann"}> Martin Hofmann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Syntax and semantics of dependent types
              = succeed "," do
                %em Semantics and logics of computation
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 14
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511526619.004"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511526619.004
          %p which originates as
          %ul
            %li#Hofmann95
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hofmann"}> Martin Hofmann
              , chapter 2 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Extensional concepts in intensional type theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.lfcs.inf.ed.ac.uk/reports/95/ECS-LFCS-95-327/"} ECS-LFCS-95-327
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/HofmannExtensionalIntensionalTypeTheory.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4471-0963-1"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4471-0963-1
          %p Texbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"}> Bart Jacobs
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categorical Logic and Type Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 141
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/bookseries/studies-in-logic-and-the-foundations-of-mathematics/vol/141"} ISBN:978-0-444-50170-7
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://people.mpi-sws.org/~dreyer/courses/catlogic/jacobs.pdf"} pdf
          %p Comprehension categories are defined in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"}> Bart Jacobs
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Comprehension categories and the semantics of type dependency
              %strong 107
              2 (1993) 169-207 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1201808"}> MR1201808
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-3975(93)90169-T"} doi:10.1016/0304-3975(93)90169-T
          %p A 2-categorical treatment of variant kinds of comprehension category is given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul-Andr%C3%A9+Melli%C3%A8s"}> Paul-André Melliès
              , Nicolas Rolland,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Comprehension and quotient structures in the language of 2-categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.10015"} arXiv:2005.10015
          %p A correspondence with orthogonal factorization systems is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Clemens Berger, Ralph M. Kaufmann,
              %em Comprehensive factorisation systems
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09438"}> pdf
              )
          %p Categories with attributes are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Cartmell"}> John Cartmell
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Generalised algebraic theories and contextual categories
                %strong 32
                3 (1986) 209–243 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0168-0072(86)90053-9"} doi:10.1016/0168-0072(86)90053-9
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=865990"} MR865990
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenio+Moggi"}> Eugenio Moggi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A category-theoretic account of program modules
                %strong 1
                1 (1991) 103-139 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1108806"}> MR1108806
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129500000074"} doi:10.1017/S0960129500000074
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+M.+Pitts"}> Andrew M. Pitts
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorical logic
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Logic+in+Computer+Science"} Handbook of Logic in Computer Science
                %strong 5
                Oxford Univ. Press (2000) 39-128 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198537816.003.0002"}> doi:10.1093/oso/9780198537816.003.0002
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~amp12/papers/catl/catl.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1859470"} MR1859470
          %p Categories with families are defined in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Dybjer"}> Peter Dybjer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Internal type theory
              %strong 1158
              Springer (1996) 120-134 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-61780-9_66"}> doi:10.1007/3-540-61780-9_66
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cse.chalmers.se/~peterd/papers/InternalTT.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1474535"} MR1474535
          %p
            and shown to be equivalent to categories with attributes in
            %a{:href => "#Hofmann97"} Hofmann (1997)
          %p
            An extension of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+with+families"} categories with families
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-truncation"} set-truncated
            categories to
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated"} truncated
            categories, called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+categories+with+families"} groupoid categories with families
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thorsten+Altenkirch"}> Thorsten Altenkirch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ambrus+Kaposi"} Ambrus Kaposi
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Szumi Xie
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Szumi+Xie"} ?
              %em The Groupoid-syntax of Type Theory is a Set
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14988"}> arXiv:2509.14988
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The formulation in terms of representable natural transformations (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+models+of+homotopy+type+theory"}> natural models of homotopy type theory
            ) is due to:
          %ul
            %li#Awodey2016
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Natural models of homotopy type theory
              %strong 28
              2 (2016) 241-286 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.3219"}> arXiv:1406.3219
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129516000268"} doi:10.1017/S0960129516000268
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Clive Newstead,
              %em Algebraic models of dependent type theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.06155"}> arXiv:2103.06155
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p A proof of initiality for dependent type theory is claimed in
          %ul
            %li
              Simon Castellan,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Dependent type theory as the initial category with families
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://iso.mor.phis.me/archives/2011-2012/stage-2012-goteburg/report.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            This was formalized inside type theory with set quotients of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            in:
          %ul
            %li#AltKap2015
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thorsten+Altenkirch"}> Thorsten Altenkirch
              , Ambrus Kaposi,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Type Theory in Type Theory using Quotient Inductive Types
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.nott.ac.uk/~txa/publ/tt-in-tt.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "https://bitbucket.org/akaposi/tt-in-tt"} formalisation in Agda
          %p Contextual categories were defined in
          %ul
            %li#Cartmell86
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Cartmell"}> John Cartmell
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Generalised algebraic theories and contextual categories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0168-0072%2886%2990053-9"} doi:10.1016/0168-0072(86)90053-9
            %li#Streicher91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Streicher"}> Thomas Streicher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Semantics of type theory – Correctness, completeness and independence results
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-0433-6"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-0433-6
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1134134"} MR1134134
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#KapulkinLumsdaine12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Kapulkin"}> Chris Kapulkin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"} Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
              %em The Simplicial Model of Univalent Foundations (after Voevodsky)
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.2851"}> arXiv:1211.2851
              )
          %p
            Contextual categories as models for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            are discussed in
          %ul
            %li#KapulkinLumsdaine16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Kapulkin"}> Chris Kapulkin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"} Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The homotopy theory of type theories
                %strong 337
                (2018) 1-38 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00037"}> arXiv:1610.00037
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2018.08.003"} doi:10.1016/j.aim.2018.08.003
            %li#Joyal17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                ,
                %em Notes on Clans and Tribes
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.10238"}> arXiv:1710.10238
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Further discussion of contextual categories is in
          %ul
            %li#Voevodsky15a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A C-system defined by a universe category
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.7925"} arXiv:1409.7925
            %li#Voevodsky15b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
                ,
                %em Martin-Löf identity types in the C-systems defined by a universe category
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.06446"}> arXiv:1505.06446
                )
            %li#Voevodsky15c
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
                ,
                %em Products of families of types in the C-systems defined by a universe category
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.07072"}> arXiv:1503.07072
                )
            %li#Voevodsky15d
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
                ,
                %em Subsystems and regular quotients of C-systems
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.7413"}> arXiv:1406.7413
                )
          %p Discussion of B-systems, another essentially algebraic model, is in
          %ul
            %li#Voevodsky14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
              ,
              %em B-systems
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.5389"}> arXiv:1410.5389
              )
          %p
            The model of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+function+types"} dependent function types
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+categories"} locally cartesian closed categories
            was first made explicit in
          %ul
            %li#Seely84
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+A.+G.+Seely"}> R. A. G. Seely
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Locally cartesian closed categories and type theory
              %strong 95
              (1984) 33-48 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100061284"}> doi:10.1017/S0305004100061284
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/rags/LCCC/LCCC.pdf"} pdf
          %p The problem with strict substitution compared to weak pullback in this argument was discussed and fixed in
          %ul
            %li#Curien
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre-Louis+Curien"}> Pierre-Louis Curien
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Substitution up to isomorphism
                %strong 19
                1-2 (1993) 51-86 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/175469.175471"}> doi:10.5555/175469.175471
                \&amp;rbrack; and: Diagrammes
                %strong 23
                (1990) 43-66 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/DIA_1990__23__43_0"}> numdam:DIA_1990__23__43_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Hofmann
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hofmann"}> Martin Hofmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the interpretation of type theory in locally cartesian closed categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Computer Science Logic. CSL 1994
                %strong 933
                (1994) 427–441 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0022273"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0022273
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p but in the process the equivalence of categories was lost. This was finally all rectified in
          %ul
            %li#ClairambaultDybjer
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Clairambault"}> Pierre Clairambault
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Dybjer"} Peter Dybjer
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Biequivalence of Locally Cartesian Closed Categories and Martin-Löf Type Theories
              = succeed "," do
                %em Typed lambda calculi and applications
              %strong 6690
              Springer (2011) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1112.3456"}> arXiv:1112.3456
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-21691-6_10"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-21691-6_10
          %p and
          %ul
            %li#CurienGarnerHofmann
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre-Louis+Curien"}> Pierre-Louis Curien
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hofmann"} Martin Hofmann
              = succeed "," do
                %em Revisiting the categorical interpretation of dependent type theory
              %strong 546
              21 (2014) 99-119 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2014.03.003"}> doi:10.1016/j.tcs.2014.03.003
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/CurienGarnerHofmann.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#LumsdaineWarren13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"}> Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Warren"} Michael Warren
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The local universes model: An overlooked coherence construction for dependent type theory
                %strong 16
                3 no 23 (2015) 1-31 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.1736"}> arXiv:1411.1736
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/2754931"} doi:10.1145/2754931
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
                ,
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ias.edu/~vladimir/Site3/Univalent_Foundations_files/expressions_current.pdf"} Notes on type systems
          %p Comparisons of various models can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Javier Blanco,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relating categorical approaches to type dependency
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/BlancoRelatingTypeDependency.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benedikt+Ahrens"}> Benedikt Ahrens
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"} Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
                %em Categorical structures for type theory in univalent foundations
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.04310"}> arxiv:1705.04310
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#KapulkinLumsdaine16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Kapulkin"}> Chris Kapulkin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"} Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                = succeed "&lbrack;" do
                  %em The homotopy theory of type theories
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00037"} arXiv:1610.00037
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Gratzer"}> Daniel Gratzer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Sterling"} Jonathan Sterling
                %em Syntactic categories for dependent type theory: sketching and adequacy
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.10783"}> arXiv:2012.10783
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benedikt+Ahrens"}> Benedikt Ahrens
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"} Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paige+Randall+North"} Paige Randall North
                (2025).
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Comparing Semantic Frameworks for Dependently-Sorted Algebraic Theories
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-97-8943-6_1"} doi:10.1007/978-981-97-8943-6_1
          %p Recent work on abstract definitions of (models of) type theory include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valery+Isaev"}> Valery Isaev
                ,
                %em Algebraic Presentations of Dependent Type Theories
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.08504"}> arXiv:1602.08504
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taichi+Uemura"}> Taichi Uemura
                ,
                %em A General Framework for the Semantics of Type Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.04097"}> arXiv:1904.04097
                )
          %p
            A category with families structure is constructed on the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,1)
          %ul
            %li#Bidlingmaier20
              %p
                Martin Bidlingmaier,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An interpretation of dependent type theory in a model category of locally cartesian closed categories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.02900"} arXiv:2007.02900
            %li#ChingRiehl2014
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Ching"}> Michael Ching
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"} Emily Riehl
                %em Coalgebraic models for combinatorial model categories
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.5303"} arXiv:1403.5303
            %li#Isaev2016
              %p
                Valery Isaev,
                %em Model category of marked objects
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.08459"} arXiv:1610.08459
          %p
            An equivalence between contextual categories and
            %a{:href => "#Voevodsky14"} B-systems
